    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d163">

                <pb facs="0293" n="199" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">367n.1121 Hanovich, Israel/15: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul at Jerusalem</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Heizer</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-05-24T20:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 24, 1924—8
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>Your May 21, 10 a.m. Pending recognition of British mandate over Palestine
                    protest should be made in any case where local courts assume jurisdiction over
                    American citizens. If further action taken against Hanovich in other than
                    Consular Court strong protest should be lodged and matter immediately reported
                    to Department.<anchor n="20" xml:id="d163-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d163-f1" n="20" xml:id="d163-f1-content">
                <p>The consul at Jerusalem reported in despatch no. 211, July 15, 1924, that
                    Hanovich had not been required to pay the fine (file no. 367n. 1121 Hanovich,
                    Israel/16).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
